<h1>smoothsort</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A variant of <a href="heapSort.html"><em>heapsort</em></a> that takes advantage of a partially ordered table.  Performance is O(n) when input is sorted and O(n log n)  performance for <a href="worstcase.html"><em>worst case</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="heapSort.html"><em>heapsort</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
From Lynn Milnes &lt;<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="8cf8fee6f9e8e3cce4e3f8e1ede5e0a2efe3e1">[email&#160;protected]</a>&gt; August 2003</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
Smoothsort, an alternative for sorting in situ <a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/EWD/ewd07xx/EWD796a.PDF" target="_blank">typescript (guarded command language)</a>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Edsger W. Dijkstra</strong>, <em>An alternative to Heapsort for sorting in situ</em>, manuscript, June 1981. <a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/EWD/ewd07xx/EWD795.PDF" target="_blank">PDF</a>.
</p>

